{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And he called it El Elohei Yisrael.</b> It is not that the altar is called \"Elohei Yisrael,\" but rather because the Holy One, blessed be He, was with him to deliver him, he called the name of the altar after the miracle, so that the praise of the Holy One, blessed be He, would be remembered through the naming of the altar — that is, \"He who is El is Elohim to me, whose name is Yisrael.\" And so we find with Moses: \"And he called its name YHWH Nissi\" (Exodus 17:15) — not that the altar is called YHWH, but rather on account of the miracle he called the name of the altar to recall the praise of the Holy One, blessed be He: \"YHWH is my banner.\" And our Sages interpreted (Megillah 18a) that the Holy One, blessed be He, called Jacob \"El.\" And the words of Torah are like a hammer that shatters a rock — they divide into several meanings, and I have come to explain its plain sense. All this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 33:20). And behold, the words of the Rav are correct regarding the plain sense of Scripture, and the meaning of \"to him\" (לו) will be like the meaning of \"and he called him Benjamin\" (Genesis 35:18), \"and one who calls you repairer of the breach\" (Isaiah 58:12). And know that this practice in Israel is indeed that they call names with praises of El, such as Tzuriel (Numbers 3:35), Tzurishaddai (Numbers 1:6) — for the one calling declares that El is his Rock (צור) and Shaddai is his Rock, and likewise Immanu El (Isaiah 7:14), and likewise the name of the Messiah, YHWH Tzidkeinu (Jeremiah 33:16), and the name of Jerusalem, YHWH Shammah (Ezekiel 48:35), and so they do with the names of angels — Gavriel (Daniel 8:16), Michael (Daniel 10:21) — for because of the greatness of their strength they declare in their names that might belongs to El and who is like Him. But Onkelos (Onkelos on Genesis 33:20) said: \"and he served upon it before El, the God of Yisrael,\" and the meaning of \"to him\" (לו) will be like the meaning of \"in it\" (בו), in the manner of \"for you choose a son of Yishai\" (1 Samuel 20:30), \"and he held on to him\" (2 Samuel 15:5), \"the waters of Merivah\" (Numbers 20:24). Or one may say: and he called — El, Elohei Yisrael — and the meaning of \"to him\" (לו) is like the meaning of \"I will go for myself to the great ones\" (Jeremiah 5:5), \"go for yourself from your land\" (Genesis 12:1). And by way of truth, it is as the midrash of our Sages that they interpreted in tractate Megillah (18a): from where do we know that the Holy One, blessed be He, called Jacob \"El\"? As it is said, \"and he called him El, Elohei Yisrael.\" And there is in this matter a great secret. They also mentioned it in Bereshit Rabbah (Bereshit Rabbah 79:8) in different language: He said to him, \"You are a god (elohah) of the supernal ones and I am a god (elohah) of the lower ones.\" They allude to what they always say, that the image (diqon) of Jacob is engraved on the Throne of Glory, and the intention is that the Shekhinah dwells in the land of Yisrael. And the enlightened will understand."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :register {:flags {0 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:39:23.163209111Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}